{"name":"Zero Gravity","hex":"#332233","fetched":"2026-10-16","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"zero gravity","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The state of apparent weightlessness which occurs in a very low gravity field, or in free fall."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/zero_gravity"}],"entries":[{"title":"Zero Gravity (Kate Miller-Heidke song)","blocks":[{"text":"\"Zero Gravity\" is a song performed by Australian singer Kate Miller-Heidke. It was released as a single on 25 January 2019, and was Australia's entry in the Eurovision Song Contest 2019 after it won the jury and public vote on Eurovision – Australia Decides on 9 February 2019. In an interview with broadcaster SBS, Miller-Heidke said she has always known of Eurovision, but became more invested since Australia became involved. She said \"I think what I love about Eurovision is the permission to go a bit bonkers and I love how it embraces all different genres and different levels of experimental music and performance. I love how theatrical it is.\""}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zero_Gravity_(Kate_Miller-Heidke_song)","wikidata":"Q61653211"},{"title":"Zero Gravity (Kerli song)","blocks":[{"text":"\"Zero Gravity\" is a single by the Estonian recording artist Kerli. Produced by Jakob Hazell and Svante Halldin, it was officially released on March 20, 2012 by The Island Def Jam Music Group on digital media stores. The song draws heavy inspiration from space tourism, air, and more prominently, sylphs, as reflected in the music video which premiered on March 22. The song is about \"merging with the spirit, becoming all love.\""}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/2/20/Kerli_Zero_Gravity.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zero_Gravity_(Kerli_song)","wikidata":"Q550589"},{"title":"Weightlessness","blocks":[{"text":"Weightlessness is the complete or near-complete absence of the sensation of weight, i.e., zero apparent weight. It is also termed zero g-force, or zero-g or, misleadingly, zero gravity."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c4/Foale_ZeroG.jpg/330px-Foale_ZeroG.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Weightlessness","wikidata":"Q4299309"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/0/0a/Kate_Miller-Heidke_-_Zero_Gravity.png/250px-Kate_Miller-Heidke_-_Zero_Gravity.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/0/0a/Kate_Miller-Heidke_-_Zero_Gravity.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/0/0a/Kate_Miller-Heidke_-_Zero_Gravity.png/250px-Kate_Miller-Heidke_-_Zero_Gravity.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AKate_Miller-Heidke_-_Zero_Gravity.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/2/20/Kerli_Zero_Gravity.png/250px-Kerli_Zero_Gravity.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/2/20/Kerli_Zero_Gravity.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/2/20/Kerli_Zero_Gravity.png/250px-Kerli_Zero_Gravity.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AKerli_Zero_Gravity.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/6/62/Kerli_Zero_Gravity_music_video.jpg/330px-Kerli_Zero_Gravity_music_video.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/6/62/Kerli_Zero_Gravity_music_video.jpg/330px-Ker